---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import katex from 'katex';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const math = (latex: string, displayMode = false) =>
  katex.renderToString(latex, { displayMode, throwOnError: true });
const payoffs = String.raw`\begin{aligned}
  U_D &= R-y[(1-p)\Delta+c_D]-k,\\
  U_Y &= R-y\Delta,\\
  V_A &= b+\Delta-c_A-xp(\Delta+F),\\
  V_R &= b.
\end{aligned}`;
const replicator = String.raw`\begin{gathered}
  \frac{dq_i}{dt}=\beta q_i(\pi_i-\bar{\pi}),\\
  \pi_{SO}=\frac{U_S+V_O}{2},\\
  i=SO\in\{DA,DR,YA,YR\}.
\end{gathered}`;
const reduced = String.raw`\begin{aligned}
  \frac{dx}{d\tau}&=x(1-x)(Ay-k),\\
  \frac{dy}{d\tau}&=y(1-y)(B-Cx).
\end{aligned}`;
const coefficients = String.raw`\begin{gathered}
  A=p\Delta-c_D,\qquad B=\Delta-c_A,\\
  C=p(\Delta+F).
\end{gathered}`;
---

<Base
  title="On the evolution of private property | Elisa Diop-Weyer"
  description="Work in progress: how land inheritance and behavioral transmission affect the emergence and stability of property-respecting behavior."
>
  <article class="research">
    <header>
      <p class="status">Work in progress</p>
      <h1 class="title">on the evolution of private property</h1>
    </header>

    <figure class="epigraph">
      <blockquote>
        <p>“The first man who, having enclosed a piece of ground, bethought himself of saying ‘This is mine,’ and found people simple enough to believe him, was the real founder of civil society.”</p>
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption><cite>Discourse on Inequality</cite> (Rousseau, 1755)</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section>
      <h2>Research question</h2>
      <p>How do systems of land inheritance and transmission of behavior from parent to offspring affect the emergence and stability of property-respecting behavior? I first study a base model in which land is allocated at random. I then extend this model to compare hereditary succession of land with random reallocation under different modes of behavioral transmission. In each case I measure how often the property-respecting strategy (defend one’s own land and respect others’; henceforth: the DR strategy) prevails, how much conflict occurs, and how long lineages retain land.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Literature review</h2>
      <p>Maynard Smith and Parker (1976) show that an asymmetry unrelated to fighting ability, such as prior possession, can settle contests. They show that the “bourgeois” rule of defending as owner and respecting as intruder can be evolutionarily stable, and that its paradoxical reverse (the “anti-bourgeois” strategy YA) can also be evolutionarily stable. Gintis (2007) uses Hawk-Dove-Bourgeois and war-of-attrition reasoning to explain how possession can be respected without third-party enforcement by linking incumbency to the endowment effect. Kokko, López-Sepulcre and Morrell (2006) couple territorial behavior to population dynamics such that the value of holding compared to the value of seeking a territory is generated by the population itself; this helps explain why owner-favoring conventions prevail. Argasinski and Rudnicki (2021) are the closest mathematical precedent to the question I address in this project; they supplement replicator dynamics with state-transition equations such that individuals carrying the same inherited strategy can be owners or intruders and move between these states. In their owner-intruder example, newborns enter the intruder population. Borgerhoff Mulder et al. (2009) connect the strength of intergenerational wealth transmission with persistent inequality across 21 small-scale societies, finding material wealth most important in agricultural and pastoral settings.</p>
      <p>This project addresses the specific question, not explicitly addressed in the papers above, of how the succession rule itself changes which conditional strategies persist. Existing owner-intruder models reassign roles at each encounter or place newborns among the landless such that inheritance of land is never separated from inheritance of behavior.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Base model</h2>
      <p>My base model assumes that behavior is faithfully transmitted from parent to offspring and that land roles are assigned at random.</p>
      <p>Each individual has one conditional strategy DA, DR, YA or YR; these are distributed in a population according to frequencies <span set:html={math(String.raw`q_{DA}, q_{DR}, q_{YA}, q_{YR}`)} />:</p>

      <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Conditional strategies" tabindex="0">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">When owning land</th><th scope="col">When not owning land</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">DA: always confrontational</th><td>Defend</td><td>Attack</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">DR: respect of ownership</th><td>Defend</td><td>Respect</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">YA: transgresses ownership</th><td>Yield</td><td>Attack</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">YR: never confrontational</th><td>Yield</td><td>Respect</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>These four conditional strategies follow the owner–intruder game of Maynard Smith and Parker (1976). The frequencies are nonnegative and sum to one. Let <span set:html={math(String.raw`x=q_{DA}+q_{DR}`)} /> be the frequency of defenders and <span set:html={math(String.raw`y=q_{DA}+q_{YA}`)} /> the frequency of attackers.</p>
      <p>Our first analytical benchmark assumes an infinite well-mixed population with no mutations. Each encounter pairs two individuals wherein each individual is equally likely to be the owner; roles are reassigned at the next encounter.</p>
      <p>Let R be the return from holding land and b the outside income of a nonowner, with land premium Δ = R − b &gt; 0. A defender wins a contested encounter with probability p such that 0 &lt; p &lt; 1 and such that the winner receives R and the loser b. An attacker pays a cost per attack of <span set:html={math('c_A')} /> plus an enforcement penalty F if it loses. Symmetrically, a defender pays a cost of defense <span set:html={math('c_D')} /> when attacked and an upkeep cost k whenever it owns, even in peace. Expected owner (U) and intruder (V) payoffs are</p>
      <div class="equation" set:html={math(payoffs, true)} />
      <p>Payoffs thus map to a per-capita reproductive rate <span set:html={math(String.raw`r_0+\beta\pi_i`)} /> with selection strength β ≥ 0. Therefore the mean payoff <span set:html={math(String.raw`\bar{\pi}=\sum_j q_j\pi_j`)} />.</p>
      <p>Hence we have the replicator equations:</p>
      <div class="equation" set:html={math(replicator, true)} />
      <p>If defensive and attacking predispositions start out independent (<span set:html={math('q_{DA}=xy')} />, and so on), this additive-payoff model preserves independence and, rescaling time to <span set:html={math(String.raw`\tau=\beta t/2`)} /> reduces to</p>
      <div class="equation" set:html={math(reduced, true)} />
      <div class="equation" set:html={math(coefficients, true)} />
      <p>For k = 0, the peaceful line y = 0 resists rare attackers whenever x &gt; B/C, but its points are not individually attracting. For k &gt; 0 peaceful defense erodes, and when 0 &lt; k &lt; A and 0 &lt; B &lt; C there is an interior neutral center at (B/C, k/A) surrounded by cycles of attack and defense. I find all equilibria for this set of differential equations and determine whether each is stable, by checking whether small perturbations such as a few rare attackers die out or grow. I plot trajectories from several starting populations for the baseline values R = 5, b = 1, p = 0.75, <span set:html={math('c_A=c_D=1')} />, F = 4, then vary k, F, p and b.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Additional parameters</h2>
      <p>I then add several new parameters and model how they affect the equilibrium of different strategies.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Modeling different institutions around the inheritance of land from parent to offspring: we have N individuals and we assume that L &lt; N identical plots (i.e. at most one per person) are assigned at random independently of the strategy one is born with. Tracking each individual’s strategy and ownership state jointly follows the method of Argasinski and Rudnicki (2021). Each generation has T rounds in which owners and nonowners are randomly paired and play the encounter above, and each round, a successful attacker takes the plot. Parents then produce N offspring, each choosing a parent with probability proportional to <span set:html={math(String.raw`\exp(\beta\pi_i)`)} />, where <span set:html={math(String.raw`\pi_i`)} /> is that parent’s average realized payoff, and all parents die. With probability h a deceased owner’s plot passes to a uniformly chosen offspring (if any); otherwise it enters a pool reallocated uniformly among landless offspring. Comparing h = 0 with h = 1, with everything else fixed, isolates the effect of inheritance.</li>
        <li>Modeling the taxation of inheritance: we assume that a government taxes inheritance such that outside income now increases with size of inheritance: an heir pays a tax τR on an inherited plot collected with enforcement probability e and the revenue is split among the landless, so outside income b rises with the amount of land inherited. τ = 0 or e = 0 recovers the untaxed model.</li>
        <li>Modeling the respect of bequests: a bequest is honored with probability w; otherwise the plot is reallocated, so the effective succession probability is hw.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Modeling different genetic inheritance systems: if we assume that each individual carries two binary indicators, <span set:html={math('g_D')} /> (defend as owner) and <span set:html={math('g_A')} /> (attack as nonowner), then the four combinations of these alleles are the four modes.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Modeling totally deterministic genetic inheritance: expressed behavior equals the indicators. Offspring have two parents, each sampled with the reproductive weights above, and each indicator mutates with probability μ (copying fidelity 1 − μ).</li>
        <li>Modeling unlinked genes: each indicator comes from either parent with probability 1/2 (free recombination).</li>
        <li>Modeling epistasis: genes are such that always confronting and never confronting are the parental combinations: the two indicators recombine with probability r &lt; 1/2, so offspring of a DA × YR pair are mostly DA or YR and DR or YA only with probability r. (Strictly this is linkage; an epistatic version would add a payoff interaction between the indicators.)</li>
        <li>Modeling behavior mimesis/cultural transmission: an offspring copies the strategy of the single parent whose plot it may inherit with copying error μ so behavior and land are inherited together.</li>
        <li>Modeling a continuous parameter for how much parental behavior affects children’s behavior but otherwise stochastic: with probability ρ an offspring copies its parent’s strategy, and otherwise adopts a uniformly random one; ρ = 1 is faithful copying and ρ = 0 removes the parent-offspring correlation.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For each setting I run repeated simulations and report strategy frequencies, attack rates, the association between strategy and ownership, and the probability that a lineage retains land, with intervals across runs.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="citations">
      <h2>References</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Argasinski, K., &amp; Rudnicki, R. (2021). Replicator dynamics for the game theoretic selection models based on state. <em>Journal of Theoretical Biology, 526</em>, Article 110540. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jtbi.2020.110540">doi:10.1016/j.jtbi.2020.110540</a></li>
        <li>Borgerhoff Mulder, M., Bowles, S., Hertz, T., Bell, A., Beise, J., Clark, G., Fazzio, I., Gurven, M., Hill, K., Hooper, P. L., Irons, W., Kaplan, H., Leonetti, D., Low, B., Marlowe, F., McElreath, R., Naidu, S., Nolin, D., Piraino, P., . . . Wiessner, P. (2009). Intergenerational wealth transmission and the dynamics of inequality in small-scale societies. <em>Science, 326</em>(5953), 682–688. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1178336">doi:10.1126/science.1178336</a></li>
        <li>Gintis, H. (2007). The evolution of private property. <em>Journal of Economic Behavior &amp; Organization, 64</em>(1), 1–16. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jebo.2006.02.002">doi:10.1016/j.jebo.2006.02.002</a></li>
        <li>Kokko, H., López-Sepulcre, A., &amp; Morrell, L. J. (2006). From hawks and doves to self-consistent games of territorial behavior. <em>The American Naturalist, 167</em>(6), 901–912. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1086/504604">doi:10.1086/504604</a></li>
        <li>Maynard Smith, J., &amp; Parker, G. A. (1976). The logic of asymmetric contests. <em>Animal Behaviour, 24</em>(1), 159–175. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0003-3472(76)80110-8">doi:10.1016/S0003-3472(76)80110-8</a></li>
        <li>Rousseau, J.-J. (1913). <em>The social contract and discourses</em> (G. D. H. Cole, Trans.). J. M. Dent &amp; Sons. (Original work published 1755)</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <p class="back"><a href={`${base}/portfolio`}>← Portfolio</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

